earch...</string><string id="terms">Terms</string><string id="definition">Definition</string><string id="acc_definition">definition</string><string id="acc_resources">resources</string><string id="acc_search_input">search</string><string id="acc_pause">pause</string><string id="acc_play">play</string><string id="acc_replay">replay</string><string id="acc_submit">submit</string><string id="acc_next">next</string><string id="acc_previous">previous</string><string id="acc_volume">volume</string></string_table></string_tables><sounds enabled="false" /><nav_data><outline><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz" displaytext="Data Center VFD" expand="true"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.6DTQSy7nl6C" displaytext="Variable Frequency Drives: How They Work and How They Can Reduce Operating Costs" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.6GJylhtjtOY" displaytext="VFD Overview" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.6PinsS0DabJ" displaytext="VFD Operation" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.6ggWzHk68jO" displaytext="Rectifier" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.6UnaYFu3Imp" displaytext="DC Bus" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.5p3Hp71iawL" displaytext="Inverter" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.5of1V4VNShA" displaytext="Inverter" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.65Tw7ti1rSS" displaytext="PWM Drive Output" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.5wa9Rn7Ljc7" displaytext="Advanced VFD Options:" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.5ZQ5NGtZtA1" displaytext="VFD Operation" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.60EXPQD6PAA" displaytext="VFD Operation" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.6Vm8Q9o2jsy" displaytext="Reducing Costs" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.5ZdCkv2X2cy" displaytext="Example" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.5rMTKABwgSM" displaytext="Some VFD Disadvantages" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6mSfonJP1wz.6Eye8SShL48" displaytext="CC BY 3.0" expand="true" /></links></slidelink></links></outline><search><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.6DTQSy7nl6C" slidebank="false">variable frequency drives: how they work and how they can reduce operating costs mission critical operations </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.6GJylhtjtOY" slidebank="false">vfd overview an ac induction motor will run at only one speed when powered from line voltage. variable frequency drives are able to change their output frequency so that a motor may be run at different speeds. most vfds take a 3ph ac input and create a 3ph ac output, but some drives may be 1ph ac input or dc input and still have a 3ph ac output. vfds are also known as afds, asds, or vsds, where “a” stands for adjustable and “s” stands for speed.  many people also refer to them simply as “inverters” or “drives.”   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.6PinsS0DabJ" slidebank="false">vfd operation variable frequency drives have three main components: rectifier dc bus inverter a speed reference signal can be external or a value stored inside the drive. an external speed reference may be from a potentiometer wired directly to the drive, or a voltage or current signal from some other device such as a programmable logic controller (plc).  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.6ggWzHk68jO" slidebank="false">rectifier the rectifier converts the ac line voltage into a dc voltage. most drives use 6 diodes to rectify the input, but 12- and 18-pulse drives are available (and also more expensive). drives are most efficient when used with 3ph input power. some drives made for 3ph input can be used with a 1ph input, but must be de-rated to 59% of their nameplate values. small drives made for 1ph input are for limited applications such as home exercise machines or residential hvac equipment. ac drives can also be purchased to run directly from a dc source so do not need a rectifier. dc drives are available for dc motors and are not interchangeable with ac drives.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.6UnaYFu3Imp" slidebank="false">dc bus the rectified voltage charges capacitors to create a dc bus voltage. the dc bus voltage is approximately the peak voltage of the incoming ac voltage. the capacitors also provide some filtering of the rectified input voltage. in applications where the drive is required to stop a load quickly, an external braking resistor may be required to prevent a bus over-voltage fault in the drive.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.5p3Hp71iawL" slidebank="false">inverter the inverter converts the dc bus voltage back to a 3ph ac voltage. the output voltage is created by quickly switching  the dc bus voltage on and off creating a pulse-width modulated (pwm) wave form.   the switching frequency is typically between 5-16khz. insulated-gate bipolar transistors (igbt) are the most common transistor used in drives for switching the output. the drive output consists of six igbts--3 for the positive voltage of each phase and 3 for the negative voltage of each phase.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.5of1V4VNShA" slidebank="false">inverter the speed of an ac motor is determined by the formula: speed =  120 x f    	          p 	where:  	f = line frequency 	p= number of poles in the motor p is built into the motor when it is made, so the only thing that can affect a motor's speed is changing the frequency to the motor. the output voltage and frequency of the drive can be controlled by controlling how quickly the igbts switch on and off. ac vfds can be used only with 3ph motors since these motors do not have internal capacitors.  motors with capacitors cannot be used because of the high switching frequency of vfds.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.65Tw7ti1rSS" slidebank="false">the image below shows one phase a drive's pwm output voltage and the resulting current seen by the motor.  pwm drive output </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.5wa9Rn7Ljc7" slidebank="false">advanced vfd options: basic vfds use a constant ratio of v/hz. when run at 60hz, the output voltage is at the rated motor voltage. when run at 45hz, the output voltage is at 75% of the rated voltage. constant v/hz keeps the motor's current and torque constant. constant-torque loads include conveyors and positive-displacement pumps. vfds can also run in constant-hp and variable-torque modes. constant-hp loads include winders and lathes. variable-torque loads include propeller and turbine pumps and fans. vfds can run open loop or use feedback encoders on the motor for closed loop control. flux-vector modes offer precise speed and torque control.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.5ZQ5NGtZtA1" slidebank="false">vfd operation vfds must be programmed to a motor and application. most vfds use a parameters list to store program information. the parameters list contains motor information and user options: motor information includes hp and full-load amps (fla) user options can include ramp-up and ramp-down times, preset speeds, and the speed reference source. advanced drives may have more parameter options and/or use  programming methods beyond their parameters list. external connections can be used for stop/start, direction control, speed reference, alarms, and emergency stop.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.60EXPQD6PAA" slidebank="false">the abb acs55 vfd has a 1ph input for residential use. digital terminal connections default to start/stop, forward/reverse, and speed source, or can be programmed by software.   an analog input for external speed reference can be v or ma. basic parameters can be set with external controls or advanced parameters can be programmed by software.  vfd operation </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.6Vm8Q9o2jsy" slidebank="false">reducing costs vfds can reduce cost by matching a motor's output more accurately to the load it is driving. motors come in standard sizes and speeds 5, 7.5, 10, 15, 20, 25, etc. horse power 900, 1200, 1800 rpm are the most common designers often have to over-size a motor to meet design criteria. using a motor that is too large for the job wastes hp wasted hp = wasted $$$  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.5ZdCkv2X2cy" slidebank="false">example a 100hp motor is used to run a pump two shifts per day.  it is 93% efficient and energy costs $0.07/kwh.  the pump uses a throttling valve to reduce flow to 80% since the motor was oversized for the application. removing the throttling valve and using a vfd to run the pump at 80% flow reduces the power used by the motor by 50%. this will save just over $7,000 per year in reduced energy costs. source: https://ecenter.ee.doe.gov/em/tools/pages/vsdpumps.aspx </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.5rMTKABwgSM" slidebank="false">some vfd disadvantages in addition to the cost of the drive itself, there are some additional costs involved with using variable frequency drives. depending on the drive's voltage and the length of the run to the motor, special (i.e. more expensive) cabling may be required. when used with a tefc motor, a drive can run the motor too slowly for the motor's cooling fan to blow enough air to cool it sufficiently, so a special (i.e. more expensive) drive-rated motor may be required. the pwm output of the drive can put harmonics on the plant's grid, which could damage or interfere with other equipment.  line filters may be needed with the drive, costing more money. bearing failure in the motor due to improper grounding is also a common problem with vfds.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6mSfonJP1wz.6Eye8SShL48" slidebank="false">adapted from content provided by karl parr, associate professor at wake tech community college. variable frequency drives: how they work and how they can reduce operating costs powerpoint by karl parr is licensed cc: by 3.0  </slidetext></search></nav_data><resource_data description="" /><transcript_data /><glossary_data /></bwFrame>